Below is a minimal, self‑contained Java snippet that shows how you would use the **`NodeIdConverter.format`** helper (exposed via the `NodeIdConverter_format` function) to append the node ID from an `org.elasticsearch.common.logging.LogEvent` into a `StringBuilder`.  
The snippet assumes that the node ID is available in the `LogEvent`; if it isn’t, the helper simply does nothing.

```java
import org.elasticsearch.common.logging.LogEvent;
import java.lang.StringBuilder;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Create or obtain your LogEvent instance.
//    (In real code this would come from the Elasticsearch logging system.)
LogEvent logEvent = /* … */;

// 2. Prepare the StringBuilder that will receive the node ID.
StringBuilder logBuilder = new StringBuilder();

// 3. Call the helper that appends the node ID.
//    The helper is exposed as a function named
//    `NodeIdConverter.format(LogEvent event, StringBuilder toAppendTo)`
//    (in the Java 8 SDK syntax).
NodeIdConverter.format(logEvent, logBuilder);

// 4. Now `logBuilder` contains the original content plus the node ID
//    (if it was present in the LogEvent).
System.out.println("Resulting log: " + logBuilder.toString());
```

### What the helper actually does

Under the hood, the helper performs roughly the following logic:

```java
public static void format(LogEvent event, StringBuilder sb) {
    // Retrieve the node ID from the event’s context map
    String nodeId = event.getContextMap().get("node.id");
    if (nodeId != null) {
        sb.append(nodeId);
    }
}
```

> **Note:**  
> * The exact key (`"node.id"`) may differ depending on how the node ID is stored in the `LogEvent`.  
> * If the node ID is missing, the helper simply skips appending anything, leaving the `StringBuilder` unchanged.

Feel free to adapt the snippet to your own logging pipeline or to wrap it in a utility method if you need to reuse it across multiple places.

